\subsection{Causal Evidence via Representation Steering}
\label{sec:steering}

The probing results establish that conflict signals are detectable, but detection does not imply causal relevance.
We test whether the probe weight direction $\mathbf{w}_{\text{probe}}$ causally influences conflict resolution by injecting $\alpha \cdot \mathbf{w}_{\text{probe}}$ into the last-token residual stream at a target layer during inference.
We measure the flip rate (fraction of samples that switch answer category) and compare against a random-direction control of equal magnitude via Fisher's exact test.

\paragraph{L13 (mid-layer, 41\% depth).}
Steering at layer 13 produces bidirectional causal effects (Table~\ref{tab:steering}).
Positive steering ($\alpha = +5$) yields a flip rate of 19.9\% versus 6.6\% for the random control (Fisher $p = 0.002$; 4 p$\to$c flips), while negative steering ($\alpha = -20$) yields 36.0\% versus 17.6\% ($p < 0.001$; 5 c$\to$p flips); both survive Bonferroni correction.
All-position injection at L13 produces identical results, confirming that the causal signal is concentrated at the final token.
Steering at nearby L16 (50\% depth, just three layers away) showed no significant effect (all $p > 0.12$), indicating a remarkably narrow causal window.
Under a conservative family-wise correction across all 30 tests (3 layers $\times$ 10 $\alpha$ values, threshold $p < 0.00167$), L13 remains significant ($\alpha = +10$, $p = 0.0003$).
An independent, pre-planned boundary-mapping replication with separately sampled data ($N = 201$, six layers from L12 to L28) confirmed L13 significance ($p < 0.001$) and localized the causal boundary: L12 showed no significant effect ($p = 0.085$), with attenuated but significant effects at L14 ($p = 0.002$) and L16 ($p = 0.005$, suggesting the original L16 null may reflect limited power), diminishing to non-significance after Bonferroni correction at L18 ($p = 0.010$) and L28 ($p = 0.284$; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:boundary}), revealing a spatially precise causal gradient centered at L13.

\paragraph{Negative layers and coherence collapse.}
Steering at L28 (probe peak, 88\% depth) produces no significant effect with either last-token or all-position injection (all $p > 0.3$); L18 (MLP peak, 56\%) is similarly negative ($p > 0.44$).
At extreme magnitude ($\alpha = +20$), the control flip rate exceeds steering ($p = 0.053$), indicating coherence collapse.

\paragraph{Detection $\neq$ Decision (Llama).}
In Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, the detectable conflict signal peaks at L28 (88\% depth), but causal influence is localized to L13 (41\% depth): late layers encode the outcome in a linearly readable form, while mid-layers host the computation that determines it.

\paragraph{Cross-model steering.}
Qwen steering at L14 (39\%, $n_{\text{test}} = 131$; probe accuracy 71.0\%) produced no significant effect at any $\alpha$ (best $p = 0.785$) and zero directional flips.
Gemma-2-9B-IT L17 (40\% depth) provides a stronger test: with 88.9\% probe accuracy---the highest among all three models---steering produced no significant change at any $\alpha$ (best $p = 0.448$) and zero directional flips ($n_{\text{test}} = 171$).
Gemma's 88.9\% probe accuracy---the highest among all three models, exceeding both Llama (76.1\%) and Qwen (71.0\%)---rules out probe quality as a confound for the negative steering result, confirming that the causal influence of probe-weight directions is architecture-dependent.
Appendix~\ref{app:steering_config} reports matched-condition results at three layers per model (${\sim}$36--43\% depth), all confirming the null.
Cross-task language probe transfer further validates this shared subspace (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

